+
+Two readings. First, the cost sits in FIRST CONTACT with a level's mechanic: r11l level 0
+takes 813 actions and level 1 takes 123 once the mechanic is known. Second, the tier-1
+explorer alone used fewer level-0 actions than the full e3 cascade on r11l, cd82 and
+sp80, and more on lp85 and su15. All single seeds, and the search is stochastic. The
+honest statement is that the LLM tier has no measured action advantage on level 0 yet,
+not that it has a measured disadvantage.
+
+Where the actions go inside one run (`r11l-2491317`, whole game, 2,077 actions):
+548 actions (26 percent) were reset-replays of already-known paths, 109 of 120
+navigation attempts fell back to reset-replay, and the forward-walk hit rate was 9
+percent. The live action-effect prior scored 29,920 candidates over 848 frontiers with
+weight 0.25 and zero errors: the affordance ranker is already on, and the agent still
+needed 815 actions.
+
+### 2.3 Accuracy: induced engines and the goal predicate
+
+| quantity | value | population |
+|---|---|---|
+| held-out exact-match of post-fix r11l engines | 0.647, 0.345, 0.639, 0.008 | 119 fresh level-0 random-walk transitions, one game, one seed (baseline note) |
+| best of all fifteen archived engines | 0.672 (pre-fix, 2026-09-02) | same |
+| planning gate | held-out 1.0; every recorded rejection is of a retained engine that scores 100 percent on its shown rows and 50 to 75 percent held out | 25 rounds, 2 runs, 2026-08-27 fine-read |
+| goal predicate fires on a real win | 0 of 31 win states across 49 engine evaluations | `results/arc_cross_level_retention_20260817/` |
+| induced goals constant over all observed frames | 89 percent; 71 of 93 live engines fail on the goal, not the dynamics | `arc_executable_world_model.py:4438` in-file measurement, 138 engines, 21 games |
+
+The engines are transcriptions of shown rows, compressed better over time and induced no
+better. A run-length-encoded case table dispatching on observed coordinates is a lookup
+table that reads like a program. The goal predicate never fires, so `plan_in_model`
+returns `None` at any budget. Every level ever won on the default scored configuration
+(LLM induction path, `CARNOT_ARC_STRUCTURED_NAV` off) was won by exploration; the opt-in
+structured-nav path is the one measured exception (tu93 level 0 to 1, REQ-ARC-WMTE-5842).
+
+One live gate observation from `r11l-2491317`, first induction attempt: the CNN ttt-prior
+engine PASSED the trust gate with `heldout_accuracy 0.0` because that gate's
+`trust_metric` is `cell_recall` (0.9086 against a 0.5 bar), and `plan_in_model` then
+expanded 20,020 nodes on an engine that never predicted a whole grid, and found no plan.
+This is not the identity trap (an identity engine has zero recall on changed cells); it is
+a recall-only metric admitting a partial engine. The cost is seconds at 4 ms per node, so
+it is not a wall-clock issue. It is a deterministic gate calibration question for that
+file's owner, not an energy opportunity; recorded here so it is not lost.
+
+### 2.4 The score arithmetic that bounds efficiency work
+
+RHAE per level is `min((human/agent)**2 * 100, 115)`. For r11l level 0, human 22:
+
+| agent actions | RHAE points |
+|---|---|
+| 815 (measured) | 0.07 |
+| 408 (halve) | 0.29 |
+| 204 (quarter) | 1.2 |
+| 100 | 4.8 |
+| 44 (2x human) | 25 |
+| 22 (human) | 100 |
+| 10.6 (Astra, 51.7 percent fewer than human, arcprize.org/blog/astra) | 115, capped |
+
+Any efficiency method that removes a fraction of a blind search's actions cannot move the
+score. The score rewards knowing what to do, which is a correct model plus a correct goal.
+That is the generation wall.
+
